  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1243 AM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nOngoing showers over the eastern plains early this morning will\nsteadily dissipate as we near sunrise, while moisture will\ncontinue feeding into the high country this morning where showers\nwill linger. Despite modestly cooler conditions today (highs will\nremain below 80F over the plains), we'll hold onto moderately \nunstably conditions and ample moisture. The latest forecast \nsoundings indicate bulk shear of 35-50 kts across the majority of \nthe plains this afternoon, which would support another round of \nnumerous thunderstorms, some of which will be strong to severe. \nThus far, there isn't much indication of low stratus this morning,\nso don't foresee convective temperatures not being reached. \nInstability will be exceedingly marginal though for areas roughly \nalong and west of I-25, so the potential for stronger storms and \nheavier rainfall will be considerably tapered outside of our rural\nplains. Hail and heavy downpours will be the primary threats from\ntoday's stronger convection.\n\nThe bulk of the stronger thunderstorms will move out of the plains\nby late evening. However, with the upper-level trough traversing\noverhead, we'll retain potential for overnight showers and weak\nthunderstorms. Increased PoPs notably for our northern areas in\nparticular. Will additionally need to monitor potential for patchy\nfog Sunday morning across portions of the lower elevations,\nespecially the South Platte River Valley.\n\nSunday stands to be our coolest day of the week with the passage\nof the shortwave. If we're able to clear out morning cloud cover\nmore efficiently, the environment would support renewed scattered\nshowers/t-storms in the afternoon and evening. It's a big if, with\nthe convective potential being notably lower for the northern half\nof our forecast area, and best for the Palmer Divide and areas\ndownstream into Lincoln County where it would be easiest to erode\nany low-level capping. Depending on how quickly the low ejects to\nour east, isolated to scattered late-day showers and thunderstorms\ncould linger into Monday, but any coverage should be notably\nreduced relative to previous days.\n\nStarting Tuesday, warmer conditions will return as a southwest\nflow pattern takes hold aloft. An embedded shortwave late Tuesday\ninto Wednesday favors an increase in precipitation potential\nnamely for our mountains, with lesser coverage/more uncertainty\nfor the lower elevations. Regardless, moisture profiles won't be\nas robust as those currently being observed, so accumulations\nshould be on the lighter side if we see any.",
